(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-12-07"] = {"phy-12-07-m01":{"idea":"A wavefront joins all the points that are at the same stage of vibration.","steps":["Points on one wavefront are in the same phase at a given moment.","Rays are lines at right angles to the wavefront.","They show the direction in which the wave travels."],"others":"Points of maximum displacement, equal speeds or equal distance from the observer are not what defines a wavefront.","trap":"Same phase, not same distance.","answer":"are vibrating in the same phase at a given instant"},"phy-12-07-m02":{"idea":"The wavefront shape depends on the source and how far away you are.","steps":["A point source sends waves equally in all directions, so the fronts are spheres.","A very distant source, like the Sun, has such huge spheres that a small part looks flat.","So a distant source gives plane wavefronts over a small region."],"others":"Plane for a point source is wrong. Spherical in both cases ignores the distance effect. Cylindrical fronts come from a line source.","trap":"Near a point source: spherical. Far away: plane.","answer":"spherical, and plane over a small region"},"phy-12-07-m03":{"idea":"Huygens' principle treats every point of a wavefront as a new source.","steps":["Each point on a wavefront sends out small secondary wavelets.","The wavelets spread forward.","The new wavefront is the common tangent (envelope) of the wavelets."],"others":"Points do not absorb the wave, do not act as mirrors, and the wavelets do not spread backwards only.","trap":"Wavelets spread forward in the direction of travel.","answer":"a source of secondary wavelets that spread in the forward direction"},"phy-12-07-m04":{"idea":"When light changes medium, its frequency stays fixed, set by the source.","steps":["In glass the speed falls to c/n.","The wavelength also becomes λ/n.","The frequency of the source does not change, so frequency is the property that stays."],"others":"Wavelength and speed both change. Direction changes in general except for normal incidence, so 'direction always' is not the property that stays.","trap":"Remember v = nλ with n the frequency: v and λ change together.","answer":"its frequency"},"phy-12-07-m05":{"idea":"Refractive index is c/v, so the speed in the medium is c/n.","steps":["By definition n = c/v.","Rearranged: v = c/n.","For glass with n = 1.5, v = 2 × 10⁸ m/s."],"others":"c/n² and c − n do not follow from the definition. nc would be faster than light.","trap":"Light is slower in a denser medium.","answer":"c/n"},"phy-12-07-m06":{"idea":"Snell's law links the sines of the angles measured from the normal.","steps":["The law is n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂.","The angles are measured from the normal.","Huygens' construction gives sin θ₁/sin θ₂ = v₁/v₂, which is the same law."],"others":"Cosines or tangents do not appear. Swapping the angles gives n₁ sin θ₂ = n₂ sin θ₁, which is wrong.","trap":"Each index goes with the angle in its own medium.","answer":"n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂"},"phy-12-07-m07":{"idea":"Reflection has two laws: equal angles, and everything in one plane.","steps":["The angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection.","The incident ray, reflected ray and normal all lie in one plane.","Huygens' wavelets on the mirror show the same result."],"others":"Incidence equal to refraction mixes reflection and refraction. A reflected ray along the normal is a special case. A half-angle rule is wrong.","trap":"Both angles are measured from the normal.","answer":"angle of incidence = angle of reflection, and both rays lie in one plane with the normal"},"phy-12-07-m08":{"idea":"A steady interference pattern needs coherent sources.","steps":["Coherent sources have the same frequency and a fixed phase difference.","Two separate bulbs have random, quickly changing phases.","So their pattern changes too fast to see. One source lighting two slits gives coherent sources."],"others":"Separate bulbs are not coherent. Different amplitudes with changing phase give no steady pattern. Different colours have different frequencies.","trap":"Young's trick: one source, two slits.","answer":"coherent, with a constant phase difference and the same frequency"},"phy-12-07-m09":{"idea":"Fringe width grows with wavelength and screen distance, and shrinks with slit separation.","steps":["β = λ D/d.","Larger λ spreads the fringes more.","A larger D spreads them more; a larger d squeezes them."],"others":"The other three put D, d and λ in the wrong places: D/(λd), λd/D and λ/(Dd) do not match the geometry.","trap":"λD over d: wavelength and distance on top.","answer":"β = λD/d, where d is the slit separation and D the distance to the screen"},"phy-12-07-m10":{"idea":"Fringe width depends on the ratio D/d.","steps":["β = λD/d.","Both D and d are doubled.","The ratio D/d is unchanged, so β is unchanged."],"others":"Twice, four times or half would need only one of D or d to change.","trap":"Check the ratio, not each quantity.","answer":"the same as before"},"phy-12-07-m11":{"idea":"Blue light has a shorter wavelength, so its fringes are closer together.","steps":["β = λD/d, so β ∝ λ.","Blue is about 450 nm and red about 650 nm.","A smaller λ gives a smaller fringe width."],"others":"Blue's speed in air is nearly the same as red's. A longer wavelength for blue is wrong. Blue is coherent at the two slits, just like red.","trap":"Violet fringes are the closest, red the widest.","answer":"blue light has a shorter wavelength, and the fringe width is proportional to λ"},"phy-12-07-m12":{"idea":"Bright fringes appear where the two waves arrive in step.","steps":["Waves arrive in phase when the path difference is a whole number of wavelengths.","Then they add up: a bright fringe.","A path difference of nλ gives a bright fringe."],"others":"Odd half wavelengths give dark fringes. Quarter wavelengths give in-between brightness. Zero is only the central fringe.","trap":"Whole λ: bright. Half λ: dark.","answer":"a whole number of wavelengths, nλ"},"phy-12-07-m13":{"idea":"Amplitudes add in interference, and intensity goes as the amplitude squared.","steps":["Each source alone has amplitude a and intensity I₀ ∝ a².","Maximum: amplitude 2a, so intensity ∝ 4a² = 4I₀.","Minimum: the amplitudes cancel, so intensity is 0."],"others":"2I₀ and I₀, 4I₀ and I₀, 2I₀ and 0 do not match 2a squared or complete cancellation.","trap":"The average is 2I₀: energy is only redistributed.","answer":"4I₀ and 0"},"phy-12-07-m14":{"idea":"With white light, the central fringe is white because all colours are bright there.","steps":["At the centre the path difference is zero for every colour.","Every colour is bright there, and together they make white.","Away from the centre the colours separate, giving coloured fringes."],"others":"The centre is not violet or red alone. It is not dark, because zero path difference gives brightness.","trap":"Central: white. Sides: coloured.","answer":"white, with coloured fringes on both sides"},"phy-12-07-m15":{"idea":"Diffraction is the bending of waves around edges and through narrow openings.","steps":["When a wave meets an obstacle or a narrow gap, it spreads into the shadow region.","The effect is strong when the size of the opening is close to the wavelength.","That is why sound bends around doors, but light hardly does."],"others":"A speed change in glass is refraction. Vibration in one plane is polarisation. Splitting white light is dispersion.","trap":"Diffraction = bending around edges.","answer":"bending of light around the edges of an obstacle or an aperture"},"phy-12-07-m16":{"idea":"Single-slit minima occur when waves from the two halves of the slit cancel.","steps":["For slit width a, a sin θ = nλ gives dark fringes.","At those angles the contributions pair off and cancel.","n = 1, 2, 3 and so on; n = 0 is the central bright maximum."],"others":"nλ/2 gives half the correct positions. cos θ is the wrong function. (n + ½)λ is the bright side-fringe condition roughly, not the minima.","trap":"Minima: a sin θ = nλ. Bright interference: d sin θ = nλ in double slits.","answer":"a sin θ = nλ, with n = 1, 2, 3, ..."},"phy-12-07-m17":{"idea":"A narrower slit spreads light more.","steps":["The first minimum is at sin θ = λ/a.","A smaller a means a larger θ.","So the central bright band becomes wider."],"others":"Narrower and 'same width' are opposite to this. Unchanged ignores the slit width.","trap":"Narrow slit, wide pattern.","answer":"wider"},"phy-12-07-m18":{"idea":"Interference fringes are evenly spaced. A single-slit pattern has a broad central band.","steps":["Double slit: the fringes are about equal in width and brightness.","Single slit: the central maximum is twice as wide as the others.","The brightness falls off quickly away from the centre."],"others":"Interference needs two sources, not one slit. Diffraction fringes are not all equal. Both patterns do not have equal fringes.","trap":"Broad centre means diffraction.","answer":"interference fringes are of equal width, but in diffraction the central band is widest"},"phy-12-07-m19":{"idea":"Only transverse waves can be polarised, so polarisation proves light is transverse.","steps":["Polarisation confines the vibration to one plane.","That needs vibrations at right angles to the direction of travel.","So light waves must be transverse."],"others":"Longitudinal waves such as sound in air cannot be polarised. Stationary is about standing waves, not polarisation. Light is not both.","trap":"Interference and diffraction do not tell transverse from longitudinal; polarisation does.","answer":"transverse"},"phy-12-07-m20":{"idea":"Malus's law: the polaroid passes the part of the amplitude along its axis.","steps":["Amplitude after the polaroid: A cos θ.","Intensity goes as amplitude squared.","So I = I₀ cos² θ. At θ = 90° no light passes."],"others":"sin²θ, sin θ and cos θ miss either the square or the right angle function.","trap":"Square the cosine.","answer":"I = I₀ cos²θ"},"phy-12-07-m21":{"idea":"A polaroid passes half the intensity of unpolarised light.","steps":["Unpolarised light has vibrations in all directions with equal chance.","On average only half of the intensity lines up with the axis.","So the transmitted intensity is I₀/2, and it is plane polarised."],"others":"I₀/4 would be too small. I₀ means no loss. Zero would block everything.","trap":"First polaroid: always one half.","answer":"I₀/2"},"phy-12-07-m22":{"idea":"At the polarising angle, tan i_p = n.","steps":["Brewster's law: tan i_p = n.","At this angle, reflected and refracted rays are at 90° to each other.","The reflected light is then fully plane polarised."],"others":"i_p is not always 45°. The forms with cos and sin do not match the law.","trap":"Brewster: tangent equals n.","answer":"tan i_p = n, where n is the refractive index of the surface"},"phy-12-07-m23":{"idea":"Glare is partly polarised light, so polaroid can cut it.","steps":["Light reflected from a flat horizontal surface is mostly polarised horizontally.","Polaroid lenses with a vertical axis block most of that light.","Useful light from other directions still gets through."],"others":"Glare is not longitudinal, not a different frequency, and not unpolarised.","trap":"That is why Polaroid sunglasses help near water and roads.","answer":"partially polarised, mostly in the horizontal plane, and the polaroid blocks it"},"phy-12-07-m24":{"idea":"A bigger objective resolves finer detail.","steps":["The smallest angle resolved is θ = 1.22λ/D.","A larger D gives a smaller θ.","A smaller angle means better resolving power."],"others":"A smaller objective worsens resolution. A longer wavelength worsens it too. The eyepiece focal length does not set the limit.","trap":"Resolving power rises with D and falls with λ.","answer":"increasing the diameter of its objective lens or mirror"},"phy-12-07-m25":{"idea":"Shorter wavelengths resolve finer detail.","steps":["Resolving power of a microscope is 2n sin θ/λ.","It grows when λ is smaller.","So using shorter-wavelength light shows finer detail."],"others":"A longer eyepiece focal length or a longer wavelength does not help. A smaller objective aperture reduces resolution.","trap":"Electron microscopes use very short wavelengths.","answer":"light of a shorter wavelength"},"phy-12-07-m26":{"idea":"Wavelength shrinks by the refractive index when light enters a medium.","steps":["λ in the medium = λ in air/n.","λ = 600/1.5 = 400 nm.","The frequency stays the same."],"others":"900 nm multiplies instead of dividing. 600 nm ignores the medium. 300 nm uses the wrong ratio.","trap":"Wavelength gets shorter in glass.","answer":"400 nm"},"phy-12-07-m27":{"idea":"Interference and diffraction are signs that light behaves as a wave.","steps":["Straight-line rays cannot explain bright and dark fringes.","Both effects need waves.","They do not tell whether the wave is transverse; polarisation does."],"others":"Sound waves also show interference and diffraction, so 'only transverse' is wrong. The ray model does not explain them. They do not show light is particles only.","trap":"Wave nature: yes. Transverse nature: needs polarisation.","answer":"both show that light has wave properties"},"phy-12-07-n01":{"idea":"Fringe width in Young's experiment is β = λD/d.","steps":["λ = 600 × 10⁻⁹ m, D = 1.5 m, d = 0.3 × 10⁻³ m.","β = 600 × 10⁻⁹ × 1.5/(0.3 × 10⁻³).","β = 3.0 × 10⁻³ m = 3.0 mm."],"trap":"Convert mm and nm to metres before using the formula.","answer":"3.0 mm"},"phy-12-07-n02":{"idea":"The nth bright fringe is n fringe widths from the centre.","steps":["Position y = n β.","n = 10 and β = 3.0 mm.","y = 30.0 mm."],"trap":"The central fringe is number 0.","answer":"30.0 mm"},"phy-12-07-n03":{"idea":"Rearrange β = λD/d to get the wavelength.","steps":["λ = β d/D.","β = 2.0 × 10⁻³ m, d = 0.25 × 10⁻³ m, D = 1.0 m.","λ = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ m = 500 nm."],"trap":"Check units: m × m/m = m.","answer":"500 nm"},"phy-12-07-n04":{"idea":"The first polaroid halves the intensity, and the second follows Malus's law.","steps":["After the first polaroid: I₀/2.","Second polaroid at 60°: I = (I₀/2) cos² 60°.","cos 60° = 0.5, so cos² 60° = 0.25.","I = I₀/8 = 0.125 I₀."],"trap":"Do not apply Malus's law to the first polaroid on unpolarised light.","answer":"0.125"},"phy-12-07-n05":{"idea":"Brewster's angle satisfies tan i_p = n.","steps":["n = 1.5.","tan i_p = 1.5.","i_p = tan⁻¹ 1.5 = 56.31°."],"trap":"For water (n = 1.33) it is about 53°.","answer":"56.31°"},"phy-12-07-n06":{"idea":"Wavelength in a medium is the air wavelength divided by n.","steps":["λ_air = 600 nm and n = 1.33.","λ_water = 600/1.33.","λ_water = 451.1 nm."],"trap":"The frequency stays the same.","answer":"451.1 nm"},"phy-12-07-n07":{"idea":"The central maximum lies between the two first minima.","steps":["Width = 2λD/a.","λ = 500 × 10⁻⁹ m, D = 1.0 m, a = 0.2 × 10⁻³ m.","Width = 2 × 500 × 10⁻⁹/(0.2 × 10⁻³) = 5.0 × 10⁻³ m = 5.0 mm."],"trap":"Do not forget the factor 2: it covers both sides of the centre.","answer":"5.0 mm"},"phy-12-07-n08":{"idea":"Resolving angle of a telescope is θ = 1.22 λ/D.","steps":["λ = 550 × 10⁻⁹ m and D = 0.10 m.","θ = 1.22 × 550 × 10⁻⁹/0.10.","θ = 6.71 × 10⁻⁶ rad = 6.71 μrad."],"trap":"1 μrad = 10⁻⁶ rad.","answer":"6.71 μrad"},"phy-12-07-n09":{"idea":"Path difference equals the number of wavelengths times the wavelength.","steps":["Path difference = 1.5 × 500 nm.","= 750 nm.","This is (n + ½)λ with n = 1, so the point is dark."],"trap":"Half-integer multiples of λ give dark fringes.","answer":"750 nm"},"phy-12-07-n10":{"idea":"Speed in a medium is c divided by n.","steps":["v = c/n.","v = 3.0 × 10⁸/1.5.","v = 2.00 × 10⁸ m/s."],"trap":"Light is slower in glass.","answer":"2.00 (in units of 10⁸ m/s)"}};
